// C#: ref is written at the call as well as in the signature, even when forwarding; receivers are plain.
// https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/keywords/ref
Grow(ref xs);
// Swift: an inout argument is written &xs, even when it is already inout; a mutating receiver is plain.
// https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/functions/
grow(&xs); xs.append(4)
// Rust: a fresh mutable borrow is written &mut xs; method receivers borrow automatically.
// https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch04-02-references-and-borrowing.html
grow(&mut xs); xs.push(4);
// Mojo: mut sits only in the signature, so the call is plain; a move into a var parameter is written xs^.
// https://mojolang.org/docs/manual/values/ownership/
grow(xs); take(xs^)
// Hylo: an inout argument is written &xs, and so is a mutating receiver.
// https://github.com/hylo-lang/specification/blob/main/spec.md
grow(&xs); &xs.append(4)

